  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e1",
      "title": "Nasal vowels",
      "body": "When a vowel is followed by n or m at the end of a syllable, the vowel becomes nasal: the air goes through your nose and you do not say the n. There are four nasal vowels. Pinch your nose: the sound should buzz."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "id": "t1",
      "title": "Four nasal vowels",
      "headers": [
        "Spelling",
        "Say it",
        "Example",
        "Meaning"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "an, en, am, em",
          "ah(n)",
          "enfant (ah(n)-fah(n)), dans (dah(n)), temps (tah(n))",
          "child, in, time"
        ],
        [
          "on, om",
          "oh(n)",
          "bon (boh(n)), nom (noh(n)), maison (meh-zoh(n))",
          "good, name, house"
        ],
        [
          "in, ain, ein, im, aim",
          "a(n)",
          "vin (va(n)), pain (pa(n)), plein (pla(n))",
          "wine, bread, full"
        ],
        [
          "un, um",
          "uh(n)",
          "un (uh(n)), lundi (luh(n)-DEE)",
          "one, Monday"
        ]
      ],
      "hear": 2
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "id": "t2",
      "title": "Say the three columns slowly",
      "headers": [
        "an / en",
        "on",
        "in / ain"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "banc (bah(n))",
          "bon (boh(n))",
          "bain (ba(n))"
        ],
        [
          "vent (vah(n))",
          "vont (voh(n))",
          "vin (va(n))"
        ],
        [
          "quand (kah(n))",
          "qu'on (koh(n))",
          "quinze (ka(n)z)"
        ]
      ],
      "hearCols": [
        0,
        1,
        2
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e2",
      "title": "Watch out",
      "body": "The vowel is not nasal when n or m is doubled or followed by a vowel: bon is boh(n), but bonne is bun. un is uh(n), but une is ün. vin is va(n), but vinaigre is vee-NEHgR."
    },
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "id": "e3",
      "title": "In Canada",
      "body": "Québec French has these nasals too, with a slightly different quality: maintenant (now) and quinze (fifteen) sound a little different from France. Both are correct."
    },
